{
  "id": "2026-10-19-dogs-bouba-kiki",
  "title": "Dogs seem to know that 'bouba' sounds round and 'kiki' sounds spiky",
  "topic": "일상",
  "level": 2,
  "voice": "en-US-AndrewMultilingualNeural",
  "sentences": [
    "Good morning!",
    "Today's story is about dogs, words, and shapes.",
    "Here's a simple test that most people get right.",
    "You see two shapes: one is round and soft, and the other is spiky.",
    "Then someone asks you which one is \"bouba\" and which one is \"kiki.\"",
    "Almost everyone says the round one is bouba.",
    "Now, a team in Italy wanted to know if dogs feel the same way.",
    "They tested seventy pet dogs while a voice played the two words.",
    "A camera tracked where each dog was looking.",
    "When the dogs heard \"bouba,\" they spent more time looking at the round shape.",
    "When they heard \"kiki,\" they paid more attention to the spiky one.",
    "To rule out any help from humans, some owners wore headphones playing rock music.",
    "That way, they couldn't hear the words, but the pattern held up anyway.",
    "The researchers are careful, though.",
    "Head direction is only a rough guess at where a dog is looking.",
    "They say more research is needed before anyone draws big conclusions.",
    "Still, the study hints that linking sounds to shapes may not be only a human thing.",
    "Maybe that's one reason dogs pick up our words so easily.",
    "Next time you see a dog, try saying \"bouba\" and see what happens!"
  ],
  "summary_ko": [
    "사람은 둥근 모양을 '부바', 뾰족한 모양을 '키키'로 짝짓는데, 이탈리아 연구팀이 개도 그런지 반려견 70마리로 실험했다.",
    "개들은 '부바'를 들으면 둥근 모양을, '키키'를 들으면 뾰족한 모양을 더 오래 봤고, 주인이 단어를 못 듣게 해도 결과는 같았다.",
    "머리 방향은 시선의 대략적 추정일 뿐이라 연구진은 신중하지만, 소리-모양 연결이 사람만의 것은 아닐 수 있다는 단서다."
  ],
